'''''Tristaniopsis laurina''''', the '''water gum''' or '''kanooka''', is a tree [[species]] native to [[Australia]]. It usually grows near the eastern coastline and along the banks of [[stream]]s, where the trunks and branches tend to be shaped in the direction of the current and give an indication of the flood height.

==Description==
''Tristaniopsis laurina'' has a slow rate of growth, and usually reaches {{convert|15|-|30|ft|m}} tall. The tree is multi-branched, and may be pruned to maintain a compact shape. It can grow to be {{convert|128|ft|m}} tall in native habitats.<ref>[[Alexander Floyd|Floyd, Alexander G.]], Australian Rainforests in New South Wales Volume 2 - 1990 {{ISBN|0-949324-32-9}}, page 176</ref>

[[File:Tristaniopsis laurina-flowers.jpg|thumb|left]]

The [[flower]]s are bright yellow and have a distinctive and, to some, unpleasant odour.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.shaman-australis.com/forum/index.php?showtopic=26262|title=Tristaniopsis laurina |date=16 December 2010 }}</ref> They attract [[honeybee]]s as well as small native species of [[bee]]. They usually bloom in the late spring or early summer.<ref name="san_marcos">{{cite web|url=http://www.smgrowers.com/products/plants/plantdisplay.asp?plant_id=1563|title=Tristaniopsis laurina (Standard)|publisher=San Marcos Growers|accessdate=2009-04-04}}</ref>

==Cultivation==
''Tristaniopsis laurina'' is cultivated as an [[ornamental tree]] by [[plant nursery|plant nurseries]], for use in gardens and civic landscaping. It is popular because it is easy to grow and is a good shade tree. Many are planted as street trees, especially in [[Sydney]].

